# E0154: type that does not conform to an interface that is needed

A type must conform to an interface when another interface inherits it, or when a bound asks for it. A bound that names a parameter of a type alias is checked at each use of the alias, on the type that the use names: with `class Heap<T: Ord>`, `type H<T> = Heap<T>` is clean, and `H<bool>` is this error at the use. So is `X<string>` for `type X<T> = T.Item`, which needs `T` to conform to the interface that declares `Item`. An interface can inherit others: `interface Shape: Named` asks each type that conforms to `Shape` to conform to `Named` too. A conformance in the list of a class or an enum meets the inherited interfaces with the methods of its body. An `extend Square: Shape` block holds the methods of `Shape` only, so the inherited interface needs a conformance of its own.

## Why

A function that takes a `Shape` can use every requirement of `Named` on it. The type must conform to `Named` for that to work. Add a conformance to the inherited interface: in the list of the class, or in an `extend Square: Named` block.

## Broken

```sisu
interface Named {
    fn name(self) -> string
}

interface Shape: Named {
    fn area(self) -> f64
}

class Square {
    side: f64
}

extend Square: Shape {
    fn area(self) -> f64 {
        self.side * self.side
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(Square(side: 2.0).area())
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
interface Named {
    fn name(self) -> string
}

interface Shape: Named {
    fn area(self) -> f64
}

class Square {
    side: f64
}

extend Square: Named {
    fn name(self) -> string {
        "square"
    }
}

extend Square: Shape {
    fn area(self) -> f64 {
        self.side * self.side
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(Square(side: 2.0).area())
}
```

## Bounds

A bound `T: Shape` asks each type argument for `T` to conform to `Shape`. The compiler checks the bound where a type argument meets it: in a call of a generic function or method, in a construction of a class or a variant, and in each type that names a class or an enum with a bound, such as the type of a local, a parameter, a field or a payload. The error points at the argument or the type that fails, and its second label shows the bound. A call that fails gives one error, and the callee's body adds none.

When the failing type is a type parameter of the function that you write, add the bound to it. The help shows the parameter with the bounds that it has, and the new one: `add a bound: T: Named & Same`. The help is not shown for an interface that takes type parameters.

A bound of a generic interface asks for its type arguments too. A type that conforms to `Convert<Fahrenheit>` does not meet the bound `T: Convert<Kelvin>`. The error names the interface with the arguments of the bound. Add a conformance with those arguments.

An interface can bound its own type parameters: with `interface Feed<T: Same>`, each type argument for `T` must conform to `Same`. The compiler checks this wherever a program writes the interface with arguments: in the list of a class or an enum, in an `extend` block, in a bound, and in the list of an interface that inherits it. A conformance that comes later in the program counts. When the argument is a type parameter, as in `interface Relay<T>: Feed<T>`, give the parameter the bound: `interface Relay<T: Same>: Feed<T>`.

## Associated types

An interface can give its associated type bounds: with `type Item: Same`, the type that each conformance chooses for `Item` must conform to `Same`. The error points at the chosen type, and its second label shows the bound. An argument of a call that is a type of `S.Item` gets its type from the conformance of `S`, so a call whose `S` does not conform to the interface gives this error once, at the argument that bound `S`. The argument that waits for `S.Item` adds no error of its own.

## Conditional conformance

A block with bounds on its type parameters gives a conformance only to the types whose arguments meet the bounds. With `extend<T: Same> Pair<T>: Same`, a `Pair<Circle>` conforms to `Same` when `Circle` does, and a `Pair<Node>` does not when `Node` does not. A call that needs the conformance, or a call of a method of the block, reports the type that fails. The second label of the error shows the bound of the function or the block that asks for it, and a third shows the bound of the conformance that the type fails.

A conformance of such a block asks for the inherited interfaces under the same bounds. With `extend<T: Same> Heap<T>: Ord2` and `Ord2: Same`, a `Heap<T>` needs a conformance to `Same` for every `T: Same`. When the type has none, the help writes the block to add. When it has one that needs more than the block gives, such as `extend<T: Ord2> Heap<T>: Same`, a label shows the bound that the block does not give, and there is no help: a second block would conform twice.

The list of a class or an enum asks the same of a block that gives it an inherited interface. With `class Stack<T>: Ord2`, `Ord2: Same` and `extend<T: Ord2> Stack<T>: Same`, the `Stack<T>` of the list has no bound on `T`, so the block does not hold for it. The error points at `Ord2` in the list, and a label shows the bound that the list does not give. Give the parameter of the class the bound, `class Stack<T: Ord2>: Ord2`, or write the conformance in the class.

## Language interfaces

`Display`, `Eq`, `Ord` and `AnyObject` are in the prelude, and a bound can name them. The compiler writes the conformance of many types (`i64`, `string`, an array of numbers, an enum of numbers), as the page for [E0152](E0152.md) says. A class has no `Eq` and no `Ord`, and an enum has no `Ord`; the help for a bound writes the interfaces that the type parameter needs: `add a bound: T: Eq & Ord`. When `Ord` is needed but `Eq` is not given, the type needs a conformance to `Eq` too, because `Ord` inherits it.

Only a class conforms to `AnyObject`. A bound `T: AnyObject` that a number, an enum or an array fails is this error, with the note "only classes conform to `AnyObject`". A type that is not a class cannot list `AnyObject`; that is [E0158](E0158.md).

## `contains`

The prelude writes `contains` for an array whose element type conforms to `Eq`, as the block `extend<T: Eq> [T]`. A call on an array of another element type is this error, at the call, and it names the element type. The label says that the bound of the call needs it, and, for an element that holds the type that has no conformance, names that type. No label points into the prelude.

An element that is a class with no written `Eq` gives "`Node` does not conform to `Eq`" for `nodes.contains(n)` on a `[Node]`, and the help says to make `Node` conform to `Eq`. Do that, or look for the element with `is`. The help is the same for an array of arrays of `Node`, an optional `Node` and an enum with a `Node` in a case: the label names `Node`.

An element of a type parameter `T` gives "`T` does not conform to `Eq`" for `xs.contains(x)` on a `[T]`, and the help shows the bound to add: `T: Eq`. The same holds for an element of `T?`, as in a `[T?]`.

An argument that is not an element is the mistake first: it is [E0032](E0032.md), and a conformance to `Eq` would not make the program right.

## Related

- [E0152](E0152.md): a conformance that a type does not meet.
- [E0158](E0158.md): a conformance to `AnyObject` of a type that is not a class.
